  "title": "",
  "body": "  Determine whether or not the following statements are true or false and then prove your answer.  .  .      We tackle each in turn.   This one is quite similar to the second statement in the previous example. It is actually true, and we can leverage the parity of to get the result we need.   The statement is true. Let , then is even or odd.  When is even, for some . Hence and so is even.  When is odd, for some . Hence and so is even.  Since in both cases is even, it is divisible by 2. The result follows.   Alternatively, we might try to prove the result by noticing that is the product of successive integers. Hence one if must be even, and the product of any integer and an even number is even, so the result must be even. That would work, excepting that we have not actually proved that product of any integer and an even number is even . Its not a hard result, but we should prove it before using it.    If we just try plugging in a few small integers, the result holds. However, we know that gets bigger much faster than does, so the result should be false when is big. Consequently we can show it is false by just plugging in a sufficiently large value of .   The result is false. We prove that the negation is true. The negation is Let then as required. Since the negation is true, the original statement is false.       "
