  "body": "    Prove that   You may use the following fact: Let be positive real numbers. Then      Recall from the definition of a limit that the proof should go like this: We're given some . We need to find some so that whenever , we have Let's unravel this inequality to see how large needs to be. We want to rewrite this inequality so that's in the form greater than something ; this something will tell us how large we need to take so that the inequality above is satisfied.  Now since, the inequality can be simplified to Because we have two different terms with in them, it may be difficult to put the inequality in the form we'd like, that is, greater than something. What we can do instead is consider which of the factors or is larger - and then only working with the larger one, by bounding the smaller one by it.  Since we know that . Dividing this inequality by (which is positive), then gives us This means that Therefore, if we show that we'll also have that Now, we can take the square root of both sides of the inequality , and use the reverse implication in the fact given in the question to say that We can actually reverse all of these steps (so we use the forward implication in the given fact), to get the following implication: This tells us that we can take to be any natural number that is larger than .  So we can now write up the proof.  But we have actually missed an opportunity to simplify things further here. Recall that by dividing the inequality by we showed that and used that to simplify the inequalities that we needed for our proof. We can simplify things further still by noting that by dividing the inequality by we get So we can use that to show Then it suffices to find big enough so that which is quite a bit easier than the mucking around with square-roots we had to do before.    First proof  Let be given. Let . Note this implies that . Now suppose that , . Since and we can use the given fact to say that and so . Moreover, since , we have , and so . Using these two inequalities, we therefore have And thus the sequence converges to 1 as required.   Simpler proof  Let be given. Let . Notice that when , we have that Since , we know that Hence Thus the sequence converges to 1.  We shouldn't feel bad that we made a more complicated proof first; It is a normal part of doing mathematics. Our first proof of a result is often bettered by our second proof.   "
