  "title": "",
  "body": "    Before completing this question, you should look over .  A sequence is bounded if there is some such that for all . If no such exists, then we say is unbounded .  We say that is increasing if for all .     Show that there is an unbounded sequence with .   Show that there is an increasing sequence with .   Show that if is unbounded and increasing, then .   Parts (a) and (b) tell us that when proving the statement in part (c), we need to use both conditions given.    For part (c), the contrapositive can help. Also, see (c).    We will prove part (c) by contrapositive. The contrapositive of the statement given in the question is So this statement is of the form . Now is either true or false   If is false, then is false, and so is true. Easy!   While if is true, then is true only when is true, and so we have to show that is true.    Also notice that this statement is of the same form as (c), and so is logically equivalent to and so is of the form . Now, either is true or false:   If is false, then is true, and so is true. Easy!   On the other hand, if is true, then is only true when is true, so we really have to show that is true.    We know one of the following is true: either is not increasing, or it is increasing. We will use these two cases to prove the contrapositive statement. In the first case, when is not increasing, the conclusion (that is bounded or not increasing) is automatically satisfied. Therefore we may focus on the second case, when is increasing. We need to show that the sequence is bounded (then the conclusion, is bounded or not increasing, will be true).  By assumption, we know that . By definition, this means there is some such that for any , there's some with . But since is increasing, we know that . While it's not necessarily true that for all , since depends on , we do know that for all . Moreover, we also know that for all , as is increasing. Combining these inequalities to get , we can show that is bounded, as desired.       We saw in part (d) of that , and is an unbounded sequence. Indeed, given , we choose be an integer strictly greater than . Using the ceiling function, we set . Then implies .    We need to find a sequence that increases, and does not go to infinity. Take . Then for all . Moreover, given any , we have that for all . This implies that .     Proof of (c)  Suppose that , and that is increasing. We show that is bounded. Since , there is some such that for any , there's some with . Fix , and let be such that . Since is increasing and , we have But as is increasing, we also know that . Let . Note that But by choice of , , and . Therefore and so . This holds for all , and so is bounded. Thus if , either is not increasing or is bounded.    "
