  "body": "Breaking a bar of chocolate  Say we have a bar of chocolate as shown below a line of blocks. Prove that it takes breaks to split it into individual pieces.   We prove this by strong induction.  First, when , there is nothing to do; it takes 0 breaks to split it into block. The result holds.   Now assume that the result holds for bars of blocks, and then consider a bar of blocks. Say we split it into two pieces, one of size , and one of size (with ). This takes break, and we are left with the problem of splitting up the bar of blocks and the bar of blocks (as shown below).   However, by assumption, we know that splitting a bar of blocks takes breaks, and splitting a bar of blocks takes breaks. So, in total we'll need breaks. Notice that this is independent of our choice . Hence the result holds for a bar of blocks.   By strong induction the result holds for all .  A slightly higher dimensional analogue of this problem involves cutting a pizza using straight slices. The interested reader should search-engine their way to the lazy caterer sequence , or (equivalently) the central polygonal numbers. Going up one more dimension one arrives at the cake numbers which define the number of chunks of cake you can make when slicing a cube with -planes.  "
