  "title": "",
  "body": "    Let , . Suppose that . Show by induction that .     The base case of our induction will be . For this part, we'll need to show that the sum of two rational numbers is rational. For the inductive step, we'll suppose that any sum of rational numbers is rational, and we need to show that if , then their sum lies in . The inductive hypothesis tells us that . But then is really the sum of two rational numbers, and hence rational itself, as we already proved this in the base case.     Let . Suppose , and that . Then there are with and so that and . Then where with . Thus .  Now assume the result holds for for some , and suppose that . By the inductive hypothesis, . Moreover, by the base case, the sum of two rationals is rational, and therefore By induction, the result holds for any .   Note that the proof by induction only gives the result for a sum of finitely many terms. We cannot conclude that the sum of an infinite number of rational numbers is rational; indeed that statement is false. To see this, suppose that , , , and in general, is the digit of . Then for all . But We'll take the statement that to be a fact, but this does require a proof, since we're dealing with an infinite number of terms. Really, we'd need to prove that which is beyond the scope of what we are trying to do in this chapter.   "
