  "body": "    Let . Prove that     Factoring a cubic can be painful. Perhaps write down the cubics you need and then expand them out. This might help with some of the arithmetic.    As scratchwork for this problem, we work through the computation we will have to do after we adopt the inductive hypothesis. That is, we want to work through the case after assuming that the case is true. By the inductive hypothesis, Expanding the expression and giving both terms a common denominator yields Grouping like terms, We now factor the top of the expression to get Finally, we rearrange into the desired form The above method works, but, in line (2), we could also notice that is a common factor, rather than expanding the cubic. That computation looks like: By the inductive hypothesis, Factoring out and giving both terms a common denominator yields Simplifying, We now factor the top of the expression to get Finally, we rearrange into the desired form     As a base case, we take . Then we see that   We assume that the statement is true for , that is and show that the statement is true for . By the inductive hypothesis, Factoring out and giving both terms a common denominator yields We now factor the numerator to get Finally, we rearrange into the desired form And so the statement holds for , as required.  Since the base case and inductive step are true, by the principle of induction, the statement holds true for each .   "
