  "title": "",
  "body": "    Prove or disprove the following statement:   If and are sets, then .     We can see that the statement says: the union of the power sets of and is the power set of their union . In other words, it claims that every set that is a subset of or must be a subset of their union and every subset of the union must be a subset of or . We can tell that the first part of the statement is indeed true, that is, every set that is a subset of or must be a subset of their union. But, second part of the statement doesn't sound right, since the union of two sets can be bigger than both sets. This would create a problem since we can simply take the union of these two sets as the subset of the union, i.e. the element of the power set of the union. This suggests that we should be able to create a counterexample as long as and . Let's see how we can create a counterexample.    Disproof  This statement is false. For a counterexample, we can take and . Then Notice that but . Consequently the inclusion does not hold.   "
