  "number": "8.6.15",
  "title": "",
  "body": "    Let . We say is an upper bound of if for every . Further, we say is the supremum (or the least upper bound ) of , denoted by , if    is an upper bound for , and   if is an upper bound for , then .  We also call the maximum element of , denoted by , if it is the largest element in . So, belongs to , and is an upper bound of .  For each of the following sets, determine its maximum and supremum, if they exist. Justify your answers.                           In order to prove that is the supremum of a set , it suffices to show that is an upper bound for , and that if , then is not an upper bound of the set. The latter statement may be rephrased as follows: if , then there is some with .  If you want to show instead that has no maximum, prove that any is not an upper bound of the set. That is, for any , there is some with .       Any real number that is at least is larger than or equal to every element of the set . That means that any real number that is at least is an upper bound of . Since is an element of , and any number less than is not an upper bound for , we see that is the least upper bound and the maximum of .    We claim that has no maximum. In order to show this, we need to show that any is not an upper bound of the set.  We claim that is the supremum of . For any , , and therefore is an upper bound of . Now we need to show that it is the least upper bound. That is, we need to show that if is an upper bound of , then . We will instead prove the converse of this statement: if , then is not an upper bound of .    It is important to realise that this is a subset of integers, not reals. Then we can simplify the set before we determine its maximum or supremum. The inequality holds if and only if Dividing by 2, we then have and then adding , Since each of these steps can be reversed, we see that this inequality is equivalent to the original one. Hence In this form, it's easier to see that the maximum and supremum of the set is : indeed, any integer that is at most must be at most , so is an upper bound for the set. Since is an element of the set, it is the maximum. Since any element less than cannot be an upper bound for the set, we see that is also the supremum.    Let   We claim that the set has no maximum. We need to show that for any , there is some other element of that is greater than ; then will not be an upper bound for , and hence not its maximum.  We claim that the supremum of is . First, we'll need to show that is an upper bound for . Then, we'll need to show that for any , is not an upper bound of . In order to do that, we need to find some so that . Rearranging this, we have . Since , we have , and so the inequality may be rearranged to . So the choice of will work.    Recall that holds if and only if for some . Hence, if , we must have for some . So We claim that this set has no upper bound; this means that the set cannot have a maximum or a supremum, since both the maximum and supremum would be upper bounds of the set.       Proof of (a)  If , then , and so is an upper bound of the set. Since , it is also the maximum of the set. Suppose is an upper bound for . Then for all ; in particular, . Since is an upper bound for , and any upper bound of the set must be at least , we have that the supremum of is .  Proof of (b)  Let . Then . Set , which is the number half-way between and . Then notice that and so . Thus and larger than . Thus cannot be an upper bound of . Since this is true for any , this set has no maximum.  Note that we could choose other -values, but this choice makes the argument quite clean.  Proof of (c)  Since this is a finite set of integers we can list out its elements explicitly and find the maximum and supremum from that. The set is equivalent to . Hence it is the set . Thus the element is the maximum. It is also the supremum, since there is no greater element, and any is not an upper bound.   Proof of (d)  We first prove that the supremum is 3 and then prove that there is no maximum.  Since for all , is an upper bound of . Now let . If , then it is not an upper bound of , since but . Now suppose . Then set to be the number half-way between and , namely set . Then, as argued above, Then but , so is not an upper bound of . Thus is the supremum of , as claimed.  Now let . Then for some . But then and so Since and , is not the maximum of . As was an arbitrary element of , the set has no maximum.  Proof of (e)  Let . We show that is not an upper bound of the set. Let . Then Since is an element of , is not an upper bound of the set. Since was arbitrary, the set has no upper bound, and hence no maximum or supremum.  After completing this question, you may be wondering when a set has a supremum or a maximum. Every non-empty subset of the real numbers that has an upper bound also has a supremum; this property is called completeness of the real numbers . This may fail if the set has no upper bound, as we saw in part (e).  However, even if a non-empty subset of the real numbers has an upper bound, it may not have a maximum. For example, in part (b), we saw that has an upper bound (for example, 3), but no maximum.  Note that rational numbers do not share this property; they are not complete. For example, the set is the set of positive rational numbers that are at most . This set has a supremum in the real numbers; it is . We will show in that . We could consider to be a subset of the rational numbers, rather than the real numbers. Then does not have a maximum nor a supremum in the rational numbers, even though it has an upper bound in the rational numbers (for example, ). With some more work, one can show that for any in this set, you can construct another in the set which is larger. In fact, we'll make that an exercise in . This statement implies that is the set's supremum in the real numbers, and that the set has no supremum in the rational numbers.   "
