  "body": "    This question involves the supremum, which we first introduced in a previous exercise, . We recommend that you complete that question before you attempt this one.  Let . We say is an upper bound of if for every . Further, we say is the supremum (or the least upper bound ) of , denoted by , if    is an upper bound for , and   if is an upper bound for , then .    Suppose that are non-empty subsets of , and , , where .   Show that .   Can you determine ?   Define . Show that .       Suppose we are trying to prove that is the least upper bound of a set . Then we need prove that the two defining properties of the supremum hold for . In order to prove the statement if is an upper bound for , then , it may be easier to show the contrapositive, if , then is not an upper bound for . In order to prove that contrapositive, we need to show that for any , there is some so that . Then will not be an upper bound for , by definition.       We need to show that satisfies the following two properties:    is an upper bound for , so for all , and    for any upper bound of .      Let's look at some examples for and to see if we can find any patterns.   Say and , so that and . Then , and so .   Suppose and , so that and . Then , and so .  From these examples, we may believe that . However, we haven't yet looked at an example where and are disjoint.   Say and , in which case and . Then . What's the least upper bound of the empty set? We claim that any real number is an upper bound for the empty set. Indeed, suppose . The statement, if then , is true, since the hypothesis is always false! Since every real number of is an upper bound, it can't have a least upper bound.      We need to show that satisfies the following two properties:    is an upper bound for .   If is an upper bound of , then .  The second part is trickier to prove, and we'll try to prove the contrapositive instead:   If , then is not an upper bound of .   This means that we need to find some such that . We want to write as a sum of two elements, say and , that are less than and respectively. If we have such an and , then they will not be upper bounds for and , respectively. Then, by definition, there will be and so that and . But then , so would not be an upper bound for .  How can we find these elements and ? There are many choices, but we'll set where . Then        Proof of (a)  Let , and , . Suppose . Then for any we know that or .   If , then .   Similarly, when , then .  Hence is an upper bound for .  Now let be any upper bound for . By definition, this means that for all , . Since all elements of and are also elements of , it follows that is an upper bound for and . Now, since and are the least upper bounds of and respectively, we must have and . Hence . Thus is smaller than any upper bound of , and so is the least upper bound of , as required.  For (b), one cannot determine the supremum of the intersection from . One requires more detailed information about the sets. See the scratchwork above.  Proof of (c)  Let , and , . Let .  Then is an upper bound for ; indeed, for any , we may write for some , . But by definition of the supremum, and . Thus .  Next, suppose that . Then is a positive real number. Hence and are not upper bounds for and respectively, by definition of the supremum. Consequently, there are some , such that and . But then and since , we see that is not an upper bound for .  Thus is the least upper bound of , as required.   "
