  "id": "sec_prop_rel",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec_prop_rel.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "9.2",
  "title": "Properties of relations",
  "body": "Properties of relations  Since a relation on is just a subset of , there are a huge number of possible relations on any given set. As noted above, the vast majority of these will unstructured and be neither useful nor interesting. Typically we require relations to have some additional structure.  Consider the relation is divisible by on the set of integers. This has some very useful properties:  For every , it is always true that ,  If and then we must have .  The relation is less than on the set of reals, on the other hand, satisfies the second of these, but not the first. These properties (and others) are useful and interesting so let's formalise them.   Let be a relation on a set .  We say that the relation is reflexive when for every .  The relation is symmetric when for any , implies .  The relation is transitive when for any , and implies .  Notice that in the definition of transitive we do not require that be different.   Notice that we can write these using quantifiers quite nicely: We can visualise these using dot-arrow diagrams (as in ). Note that these diagrams are not supposed to represent the entire relation, but rather just enough to illustrate the definitions.  Reflexive:   Every element must have a loop from back to itself.   Symmetric:   If there is an arc from to then there must also be an arc from to .   Transitive:   If there is an arc from to and another from to then there must also be an arc from to .   With a little work (and some proofs!) we can show that the following table holds for the relations on the set of integers.    R           Reflexive false true true true    Symmetric false false true false    Transitive true true true true      Let be the relation has the same parity as on the set of integers. This relation is defined by Notice that the second definition is equivalent to the first since Prove that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.    We explore each in turn:  Reflexive. We need to show that for every single that . Hence we need to show that for every integer , that has the same parity as . This is pretty obvious, but to write it a little more mathematically since and , we know that .  Symmetric. We need to show that if then . So we start by assuming that , so . We need to show that . This, again, is quite obvious, but we can make it more mathematical by saying something like since we know that , so , and thus   Transitive. We need to show that if and then . So we assume that and . This means that and . So there are integers so that Now we need to show something about . It is easy to isolate and , so . Hence as we need.  We are not done until we write it up nicely. So we do that now.     Let the relation be as defined in the statement. We prove each property in turn.  Reflexive Let . Since and , we know that .  Symmetric Let so that . We know that and so we can write for some . But then , and so and thus .  Transitive Let with and . Thus we know that and for some integer . But then and thus . Hence as required.       More examples:   Let be the set of students at UBC, and consider the relation attended highschool with .  Reflexive It is reflexive since every student went to highschool with themselves.  Symmetric It is symmetric, because if student went to highschool with , then went to the same highschool as .  Transitive It is not transitive, just because went to school with and went to school with , it does not mean that went to school with . It is possible that went to two different highschools, one they attended with and the second they attended with .    Another student flavoured example.   Let be the set of students in the student union building at 1pm, and let be the relation is within 2 metres of .  Reflexive It is reflexive since each student is less than 2m from themselves.  Symmetric It is symmetric, because if student is less than 2m from student , then is less than 2m from .  Transitive It is not transitive, just because is 2m from and is 2m from , it does not mean that is 2m from . If the students are arranged in a line with 1.5m between each of them, then is 3m from .     Let be the relation is a subset of on the set of all subsets of the integers.  Reflexive The relation is reflexive since for all sets , we have .  Symmetric The relation is not symmetric. Let , then but .  Transitive The relation is transitive. Assume and . Now let . Since we know . And since , we know . Hence as required.    These aren't the only interesting properties of relations. Here are some others we won't really use, except maybe for some examples and exercises.  A relation is total (also called connex ) when The relation on the set of reals is total.  A relation is trichotomous when The relation is trichotomous.  A relation is anti-symmetric when The relation is anti-symmetric, so is on natural numbers. However is not anti-symmetric on the set of all integers. If , then and , but .  A relation is dense when     One very important relation on the integers is congruence - recall . It is not hard to prove that congruence has nice properties.   Let then congruence modulo is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.    Let be a fixed natural number. We prove each of the properties in turn.  Reflexive Let . Then since and , we know that .  Symmetric Let and assume that . Hence we know that , and so we can write for some integer . Thus and so and .  Transitive Let and assume that and . This implies that and , and so we can write for some integers . Consequently and . Hence .     Notice that one immediate consequence of this theorem is that since congruence modulo is symmetric, we can be a little bit more relaxed when discussing it. In particular, in the examples above we have been very careful to say  is congruent to modulo 7  is congruent to modulo 4  is not congruent to modulo 5  where there is a definite first number and a definite second number in the relation. However since congruence is symmetric, we can instead say  and  are congruent modulo 7  and  are congruent modulo 4  and  are not congruent modulo 5  where the order of the two numbers in the relation no longer matters. Of course we must still be careful with the modulus; we cannot mix that up with the other numbers.  We will return to congruences in more detail shortly.  Another example:  Fractions  Let be the set of all fractions: Consider the following fractions that are different elements of : All of these are just different ways of writing one half and so correspond to the same single element of . We typically avoid this duplication by representing each rational number by a single reduced fraction. This is an example of an equivalence class , but we will get to those in the next section.  But before that, let us formalise how two fractions are related. We've been doing that since primary school they are related when they are the same rational number. That is Notice the in the equation on the right does not mean that the fractions are identical rather it means that they represent the same number. We can make this condition a little more comfortable by writing it as Let us show that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.   We show that the relation on the set of fractions defined above is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.   Reflexive Let . Then since , it follows that . Hence the relation is reflexive.   Symmetric Let . Assume that . Hence , and so . Thus . So the relation is symmetric.  Transitive Let . Assume that and . Hence we know that Multiply the first of these equations by and the second by . This gives Using the transitivity of equality we know that and since we have Thus and so the relation is transitive.      "
