  "body": "Fractions  Let be the set of all fractions: Consider the following fractions that are different elements of : All of these are just different ways of writing one half and so correspond to the same single element of . We typically avoid this duplication by representing each rational number by a single reduced fraction. This is an example of an equivalence class , but we will get to those in the next section.  But before that, let us formalise how two fractions are related. We've been doing that since primary school they are related when they are the same rational number. That is Notice the in the equation on the right does not mean that the fractions are identical rather it means that they represent the same number. We can make this condition a little more comfortable by writing it as Let us show that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.   We show that the relation on the set of fractions defined above is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.   Reflexive Let . Then since , it follows that . Hence the relation is reflexive.   Symmetric Let . Assume that . Hence , and so . Thus . So the relation is symmetric.  Transitive Let . Assume that and . Hence we know that Multiply the first of these equations by and the second by . This gives Using the transitivity of equality we know that and since we have Thus and so the relation is transitive.     "
