  "title": "",
  "body": "    Let be the relation on defined by where .     Show that is an equivalence relation    Let be the set of equivalence classes of the relation defined in part (a). Let be defined by Prove that    is a function, and, further    is bijective.            What do the equivalence classes of look like?   How can we show that is, indeed, a function?      When we look at the relation defined in the question, we can easily see that it is reflexive (since equality is reflexive), and symmetric (since equality is symmetric). Moreover, if and , we see that . Therefore it also is a transitive relation. As we can see, trying to show that this relation is an equivalence relation is not too hard to show.  Now, let's try to understand the equivalence classes of this relation. Let , then we see This means that is the set of all points on the plane that have the same distance from the origin, that is, the points on the circle, centred at origin of radius .  This is an important observation in understanding whether is a function. We know that for to be a function, we need to show that    is defined on all its domain this is easy since for any we know that and so as required.   if we input different representatives of the same equivalence class, we get the same result, namely We also know that if , then the points and are on the same circle centred at the origin. Then, since takes an equivalence class (which is a circle) and sends it to the radius of the circle, we see that it shouldn't matter which points on the circle we pick as the representative of the equivalence class, we get the same output for .    This means that defines a function.  Once we see that is a function we can also easily see that it is surjective since for all , we can take , and see that To show the injectivity we remember, again, that takes the circles centred at the origin and sends them to their radius. This suggests that if then the radius of the circle defined by and must be the same. Since these circles are centred at the origin, we see that they must be the same, that is, which means that is injective.    Let be the relation on defined as      We need to show that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.   {Reflexivity:} Let . Then, we see that since , we get . Hence is reflexive.    {Symmetry:} Let and assume that . This means . Then, we see that . Hence, we see that , that is, is symmetric.    {Transitive:}. Let and assume that and . This means and . Then, we see that . Hence, we see that , that is, is transitive.   Hence the relation is an equivalence relation.    Now, let be the set of equivalence classes of the relation defined in part (a) and define , defined by       Show is a function: Let be an equivalence class. Then since we know that and so . Hence is defined everywhere on its domain. Now let . This means that . Thus, we get Therefore, defines a function.     Show is bijective: We need to show that is injective and surjective.      Injective: Let , and assume that This means that , which in turn implies that Thus, we get , that is, Hence is injective.     Surjective: Let . Then, we can take , and see that Hence, is surjective.  Therefore, we see that is bijective.         "
