  "number": "11.3.6",
  "title": "",
  "body": "    Prove each of the following statements:   There is no smallest positive rational number.   There is no smallest positive irrational number.       Try adapting the proof of .       We'll prove the statement by contradiction, and assume that there is some smallest positive rational number, say . We'll try to find a positive rational number that's strictly less than . Let's consider , which we know satisfies the inequality . This inequality isn't enough though; we also need to show that is a rational number, and then we'll have reached a contradiction.    The proof is very similar to part (a). We'll assume that we have a smallest positive irrational number, say . We'll try to show that is irrational. We can prove this statement by using contradiction as well: assume that is rational, and show this contradicts the assumption that is irrational.       Proof of (a)  Assume, for a contradiction, that is the smallest positive rational number. Then there are , , such that . Thus and as with , we have that is rational. But as , so is a positive rational number smaller than . This contradicts our assumption on , and so there is no smallest positive rational number.  Proof of (b)  Assume, for a contradiction, that is the smallest positive irrational number. We claim that is also irrational. If this were not the case, then there would be with such that . But then and as with , we have that is rational. This is a contradiction, and so must be irrational. Since , so is a positive irrational number smaller than . This contradicts our assumption on , and so there is no smallest positive irrational number.   "
