  "title": "",
  "body": "    Recall the Intermediate Value Theorem:    Let where . Suppose is continuous on , and then there exists such that .   Let be a continuous, bijective function. Using the Intermediate Value Theorem, prove that is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing. See for the definitions of strictly increasing and decreasing.    What would happen if the function weren't strictly increasing or decreasing?    Let be a continuous, bijective function.  By way of contradiction, assume that is neither strictly increasing, nor strictly decreasing.  Then there exists with such that    and (the function increases and then decreases). Or,    and (the function decreases and then increases).     First, we consider case (1). In this case, the function increases and then decreases, but we do not have information about how compares to . This gives us two further sub-cases.   Suppose . We combine this with the inequalities from (1) to find We can regard as the value in the statement of the Intermediate Value Theorem. Since is continuous, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists such that . Because , . This contradicts the assumption that is injective.   Suppose . Combining this with the inequalities from (1) yields We can now regard as the value in the statement of the Intermediate Value Theorem. Since is continuous, the Intermediate Value Theorem tells us that there exists such that . Because , and we find that is not injective.     The argument is similar for case (2). We break into two further sub-cases which compares the value of with .   Suppose . Then the inequalities from (2) tell us We regard as the value in the statement of the Intermediate Value Theorem. Since is continuous, the Intermediate Value Theorem says there exists such that . Notice that , so . We conclude that is not injective.   Finally, suppose . is continuous, and from (2) we know that We regard as the value in the statement of the Intermediate Value Theorem. By the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists such that . Because , we see that , so is not injective.   In each of the four cases, we reach the conclusion that is not injective, contradicting our assumption that is bijective.  We conclude that must be strictly increasing or strictly decreasing   "
