  "body": "More comparisons of cardinalties   At this point, we have given extra meaning to the symbols and ' ' so that they can be used to handle cardinalities of infinite sets. In that context do they still behave the same way as they do in the context of (say) comparing real numbers? So, for example, does the following hold:   if and then    This is equivalent to   If there is an injection from to and an injection from to , then there is a bijection from to .    This result is the Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein This was first published without proof by Cantor in 1887. In the same year it was proved by Dedekind, but not published. Schröder gave a proof sketch in 1896, but it was found to be incorrect. Then in 1897, almost simultaneously, Schröder and Bernstein gave correct proofs. At this time, Bernstein was 19 years old! Dedekin proved it again later that year. Why Dedekin doesn't get more credit for this theorem remains mysterious. Theorem   The Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem  Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein (and also Dedekind)  Let be sets. If there is an injection and an injection , then there is a bijection . Equivalently, if and then .   The proof is by no means trivial; we have some work to do. First up let us assume that we have those injections and .   We can think of injecting a copy of into  namely . It is not hard to show that by restricting the codomain we can build a function is a bijection. So that looks like a good place to start making our bijection from to . We can similarly restrict the codomain of to construct Since this is also a bijection, its inverse is a bijection giving us another bijection This should give us some hope because by restricting codomains we have  a bijection, , from all of to some (but not all) of , and  a bijection, from some (but not all) of to all of .  So perhaps we can build a bijection from all of to all of by carefully choosing between these two depending on our input.  To investigate things a bit further, we should think about what happens when we compose and . Let and consider the image of under  we get some element . We cannot do much with this, but we can apply to it, giving us . This, in turn, is some element of , so we can apply to it, etc etc. In this way, we can think of the trajectory of under these two injections. What we have here is the forward trajectory of under and .   But we could also go backwards. For any , we can compute the preimage of . Since is injective, we know that that this set is either empty or contains precisely 1 element (otherwise would fail to be injective). We can make a similar argument about any , its preimage is either empty or contains exactly 1 element. Hence we can extend this trajectory backwards via unique preimages unless we get stuck at an element whose preimage is empty.   Each element has a unique forward step along its path by applying or as appropriate. Similarly, when we step back along the trajectory, we move to a unique element (due to injectivity) by application of or , or the preimage is empty and cannot move any further back. So two trajectories cannot merge, nor can a trajectory branch:   Because of this, the trajectory of any point can be of 4 types:  the trajectory forms an infinite line  the trajectory is a loop  the trajectory goes forward forever, but going backwards we get stuck at an element of , or  the trajectory goes forward forever, but going backwards we get stuck at an element of .     We cannot have more complicated shapes. Let us call the first three types good and the last type that gets stuck at   bad .  Now, define a function by To see that this is actually function take any element of . We can always form , since is a function. If lies on a bad trajectory, then we know we can go backwards until we get stuck at some element of  thus the preimage of must contain exactly 1 element which we take to be .  Oof. Now we just have to prove this is a bijection.  is a surjection  Let . Examine the trajectory on which lies it is either good or bad .  If lies on a good trajectory, then (by moving back one step along the trajectory) there is some so that . This must also lie on a good trajectory and so .  If lies on a bad trajectory, then (by moving forward one step along the trajectory) we set . We know that also lies on a bad trajectory, so .  In either case, by moving forward or backward one step along the trajectory of we find an so that .   is an injection  Let , and assume that . Notice that by construction, lies on the same trajectory as . Thus and must all lie on the same trajectory. That trajectory is either good or bad.  If the trajectory is good, then and and so . Since is an injection, we know that .  If the trajectory is bad then and . So  since is an injection, that preimage contains exactly one element, so we must have .  In either case, we have that .    Applications  The Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem makes proving the following results much easier.   The sets and are all equinumerous.    By Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein it suffices to construct injections from to and vice-versa to show that they are equinumerous.   Let be defined by . This is an injection since if then .    Let be defined by (there are many similar choices). Again, this is an injection since if then .     We can then show that the second and third sets are equinumerous via the explicit bijection This is injective since if then so . It is surjective since for any pick . Then as required.  Finally we can prove that the first and last sets are equinumerous by very similar injections to those we used to prove that the first and second sets are equinumerous.   Let be defined by . It is (immediately) injective by the same argument used above.    Let be defined by . It is injective by similar arguments.   So by CSB we have that .   This can also be proved without CSB, but it is definitely more work we'll demonstrate that the first two sets are equinumerous. The approach is to split the set into the set and everything else. Then things of the form are mapped to , so that This way we can map something to 0 while not forgetting or missing any other element.   Let be defined by We then need to show that this is both injective and surjective.   Injective Let and assume . If for some , then we must have . On the other hand if is not of this form then , so .    Surjective Let . If for some , then set . Otherwise set . In either case as required.       Proof that  From Cantor's theorem we saw that , and by Cantor's diagonal argument we also saw that . It is not unreasonable to ask to compare these two uncountable sets. The following really beautiful result shows that they are actually equinumerous.    The sets and are equinumerous.     By the Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem it suffices to construct injections from to and vice-versa.   We define an injection . Let , we can then construct by constructing its decimal expansion. In particular, write , and then Now if we take with , then   there is so that , or    there is so that , or both.   In either case, this means that corresponding expansions differ at either or , and so .    It is easier to construct an injection from to rather than from to . However, since we have proved that , we know there is a bijection between and . So if we compose that bijection with the injection we are about to construct, we get the required injection from to .  Given any we write its decimal expansion as . As noted above we can make this expansion unique by avoiding any expansion that ends in an infinite sequence of 9's. Using this expansion we can define by So, for example, Now if then their decimal expansions must differ at least one digit. Consequently their images will be different subsets of . Hence the function is an injection from to , and so there is an injection from to as required.       "
