  "body": "(Optional) Cantor's first proof of the uncountability of the reals  For completeness we include Cantor's first proof that . This, proved in 1874, is more involved than his very famous diagonal argument which was published in 1891. Despite this it is still accessible to the tools developed in this text.  Like the diagonal argument, this first proof also is a proof by contradiction. We assume the existence of a bijection from to an interval of the real-line and then show that this leads to a contradiction. However, rather than relying on decimal expansions of real numbers, it instead relies on the supremum the least upper bound property. The reader should take a moment to examine and , and also revise before continuing.  Least upper bound property of the reals   Let be bounded above. That is, there is some so that for all . Then the supremum of exists and is a real number.    This is the critical difference between the reals and the rationals the rationals do not satisfy the least upper bound property. For example, one can take the set of truncated decimal expansions of : The least upper bound of this set is which is not a rational number .  We can use the least upper bound property to prove This is a nice and intuitive result; in fact it is such a nice result we set it as an exercise . that increasing sequences of real numbers that are bounded above must converge to a real limit.    Let be a sequence of real numbers that  is bounded above by some real number , and  satisfies for all .  Then the sequence converges as .    See .                      Now we are ready to get into the proof. Let be an interval of the real line with . Then assume to the contrary, that there is a bijection This bijection defines an infinite sequence via for any . Notice that all the terms of this sequence must be distinct since is injective.  The proof works by finding some so that is not part of the sequence. Hence there is no so that , and so is not surjective and hence not bijective.  We construct two new sequences and from the sequence .  Find the first two lying strictly inside the interval, that is . These two terms must be distinct and let be the smaller and be the larger.  Similarly, find the first two terms of the sequence that lie inside  call the smaller and the larger .  Keep repeating this to define , and so on.  Notice that  for any we must have , and  the sequences of 's and 's satisfy and so the sequence is increasing and bounded above by , while the sequence is decreasing and bounded below by .   the sequences of 's and 's may or may not be infinite.  for any , the terms do not lie inside the interval .  This last point can be proved quite readily using induction on .    The interval does not contain the sequence terms .    We prove the result by induction on .  Notice that must belong to (by construction). Thus since the open interval exludes the endpoints.  Now assume that the result holds for , and so are lie outside the interval . Then either lie outside this interval, or, if they lie inside the interval they are one or both of . Consequently they lie outside .  Hence the result holds for all .    The sequences may or may not be infinite. So initially assume that they are finite. That is, the final interval is . Now notice that there cannot be two or more inside the interval because otherwise we would use those to define another interval . Thus there are either zero or one more term of the sequence lying inside . But this means that any other number inside is not part of the sequence . That is, there is some so that for any .  Now instead, assume that the sequences of 's and 's are infinite. Since the sequence of 's is increasing and bounded above by , implies that it converges to a limit. The same argument (applied to ) shows that the sequence of 's also converges to a limit. So define Notice that we cannot have (otherwise we would have for some ), and so either or .  If , then every is not in the sequence of 's. If for some , then this would contradict above, since it would imply that .  On the other hand, if , then is not in the sequence of 's. If for some , then again this would contradict since it would imply that .  Thus even when the sequences of 's and 's are infinite, there is always some that is not in the sequence of 's. Hence the function is not a surjection, contradicting our initial assumption that it is bijective.  "
