  "body": "    Let be any nonempty sets. Suppose that and , and that and . Show that .    The sets do not have to be finite.    In this question, we intuitively know that the result must be true. This is because we realize that if all the sets are finite, the result is more or less trivial. When the sets are finite we know that the we can equate the cardinality of a set with its size , i.e. the number of elements it has. This means that if and , we can conclude that and have the same number of elements and and have the same number of elements. Thus, since and , we get and the result follows.  Unfortunately, this argument fails when at least one of the sets is infinite, since we cannot talk about the sizes of infinite sets. So what we need to, instead, is to use the actual definition of two sets having equal cardinalities. That is, we know that and means that there exist two bijective functions and .  Now, we need to be able to find a bijective function For this, we realize that if , then or . This means that for each case, we can use either or to send to , which will define our function . Then all we will have to do is to show that this new function is indeed bijective.     Since and , there are bijections and . Since , any , either or but not in both. Define by And since this is well defined.  We now show is bijective. To see is injective: assume .    . So hence as is injective.    . So hence as is injective.    and (or similarly and ), and . But , which gives us a contradiction. So this case cannot happen.   Since we always have , we conclude is injective.  To see is surjective: For any , %since , or but not in both.    . There is s.t. since is surjective. So .    . There is s.t. since is surjective. So .   We conclude is surjective. Hence is bijective and .   "
