  "body": "    Let and be sets. Let be a partition of , and let be a partition of . Suppose that we have a bijection between the partitions, , with the additional property that for every set .  Prove that the underlying sets, and , have the same cardinality.       The function is not a from to , it is a function from the partition to the partition .   What does it mean for and have the same cardinality?    may be useful.      We saw in that if are any nonempty sets satisfying and , and that and , then we have . The idea in the proof relied on the fact that since and , there are bijections and . Using these functions, we could construct a piecewise function that turned out to be a bijection as well.  We can see this result as a specific example to this question, where our partitions for the sets and are given as   and   Then, we see that , defined as , and is a bijection from to , and moreover and .  This suggests that if we have partitions with more sets in them, we should be able to construct a bijection in a very similar piecewise manner. Also, since sets in a partition are mutually disjoint, and that their union gives the entire set, such a construction would still work.     For each , since and have the same cardinality, there exists a bijection . Define to be the union of all these : Of course, it is not immediately obvious that this is a function, let alone that it is a bijection. We must prove that.  We show that is a function from to , that it is surjective, and that it is injective.   For any , choose such that and . Since and is a partition of , we know that , and therefore . Similarly, and ; since is a partition of , we see that , and so . This shows that .  Let be arbitrary. Since is a partition of , there is a unique with . Since is a function from to , there is a unique such that . This shows that there is a unique with , and so is a function.    Let be arbitrary. Since is a partition of , there exists with . Since is surjective, there exists with , and therefore is a function from to . Since is surjective, there exists such that . By definition, we see that . Therefore is surjective.    Let , and suppose that . Since is a partition of , there is a unique with . Since is injective, there is a unique with . This corresponds to the only function whose codomain is , and so we must have and and . Since is injective, we conclude that . Therefore is injective.      "
