  "number": "12.7.20",
  "title": "",
  "body": "    Consider the following questions about countable unions of countable sets.   Let be denumerable sets, and suppose that whenever . Show that is denumerable as well.   Now suppose are countable sets, and suppose that whenever . Show that is countable as well.   Redo part (b), but without the assumption that whenever .       Try using the diagonal sweeping argument given in the proof of . For part (c), try defining new sets such that and for . Then apply part (b).       Since each is denumerable, we can list the elements of each set, like We can then construct an infinite table, where the row of the table lists the elements of . Then, just as in the proof of , we can construction a bijection from the natural numbers to the elements of the table by sweeping over successive diagonals.    This part differs to part (a), as the sets may be finite or denumerable. In particular, it's possible that some of the are the empty set.  Initially, suppose that only finitely many of the sets are non-empty. We'll return to the other possibility shortly. By relabeling if necessary, we may assume that the first sets, are non-empty, and for . Then We are now only taking the union over a finite number of sets, and we need to show that it is countable. tells us that the union of two countable sets is again countable. To get that the union of countable sets is also countable, we'll need to apply induction.    We would like to define sets such that and for . That way we may apply part (b) to get the desired result.  Let . Then, let consist of all elements of that are not also elements of . That way , and , by construction. Next, we would define to be all elements of that are not elements of or . Another way of writing this is We proceed in this manner to define for all . We need to show that the union of all the sets is equal to the union of all the sets , and that the sets satisfy the conditions of part (b).       Proof of (a)  Let be denumerable sets, and suppose that whenever . Since each is denumerable, we have We can construct the following infinite table: Define the function as and so on. Since every element is eventually reached (it will be reached after a finite number of diagonal sweeps, and each of these diagonal sweeps has finite length), is surjective. The assumption that whenever implies that there are no repeated elements in the table. The diagonal sweeping counts every element in the table only once, and so is injective.  Proof of (b)  We claim that is countable for each . This is the case for , by assumption. Suppose that it is true for . Then is the union of two countable sets, and so countable itself, by . Thus the result holds for . Thus by induction, the result holds for all , and in particular, for .  Now assume that there are infinitely many sets that are non-empty. By removing the empty sets, and relabeling if necessary, we may assume that for all . The argument to prove that the union of these sets is countable is similar to part (a). We can construct a table with the row listing the elements of . There is at least one element in every row, but some rows may have only finitely many elements.  We can construct a bijection by sweeping over diagonals of the table. However, some positions in the table may be empty. For example, suppose that and , and the table looks like Then would be given by and so on and so on. Notice that the first column of our table contains no empty spaces, because each set is non-empty. We need to make sure that we don't pass over infinitely many empty spaces in the table one after another. If this were the case, would be defined for only finitely many , and we wouldn't reach every element in the table. Indeed this cannot happen. Each diagonal sweep starts from a (possibly empty) spot in the topmost row and moves to the leftmost column in a finite number of steps. Since the leftmost column contains no empty spaces, each diagonal sweep reaches a non-empty space in the table after a finite number of steps.   Proof of (c)  Let be countable sets. Let . For , let We claim that and that for . First we show (1). Suppose that Let Then exists, since is non-empty (as is an element of the union, so must be in some ), and a subset of the natural numbers (so it has a minimum). Then as for by choice of . Combining this with the fact that , we have   Therefore Conversely, suppose Then there is some such that implying that . Thus (1) is established.  Now we show that the sets are disjoint. Let . Without loss of generality, we may assume that , as we may relabel if necessary. Then So, when choosing elements of to include in , we leave out any elements that appear in . That is, is empty.  Finally, for all , , and so . Since is countable, is countable as well.  By part (b), is countable, and hence by (1), is countable as well.   "
